  "response": "Scholars contrast diffusion from a single source with independent invention when explaining a trait shared by two distant societies, and the history of the discipline still supplies the terms in which that contrast is argued. The Kulturkreis diffusionists traced most cultural traits to a small number of originating centers, a position that cannot be squared with the unilineal evolutionists' claim that societies pass through similar stages and invent similar traits independently; the two accounts cannot both be true. The evolutionists advanced their scheme first; only afterwards did Franz Boas reject diffusion and explain cultural similarities solely by independent invention. Whereas the Kulturkreis school concentrated the origins of culture in a few favoured centers, Boas's account allowed no transmission between peoples whatever.\n\nIn practice, adjudication begins with the plausibility of transmission itself. A diffusion hypothesis requires a demonstrable route of contact between the two societies, a corridor of trade, migration or navigation along which the practice could actually have travelled. Because such a corridor must be exhibited rather than taken for granted, the absence of a trait in intervening regions weakens a diffusion claim, for a practice that moved should have left deposits along its path. The age-area hypothesis, by contrast, holds that the region where a trait appears most recently is its center of origin, and distributional reasoning of this kind has long been used to convert maps of occurrence into narratives of movement.\n\nDistribution alone, however, does not settle the matter, since the character of the resemblance is itself diagnostic. Because convergent functional constraints can produce similar technologies in societies that never met, shared arbitrary details, such as ornament, nomenclature, taboo or ritual sequence, thereby come to carry more evidential weight for common origin than shared functional features do.\n\nSince arbitrary resemblance counts for so much more than functional resemblance, loanwords accompanying a practice support its transmission from one society to the other. Comparative linguistics is accordingly among the instruments the problem demands, and the sound-correspondences of a borrowed term can be traced with a precision that artefact typology rarely allows.\n\nA 2020 genomic study published in Nature reported admixture between Polynesians and Native Americans around 1200 CE, a finding that shows ancient DNA can establish population contact independently of material culture. That report came after sweet potato chronology had already framed the Pacific question as a stark either/or: either the remains recovered in Polynesia predate 1492, or every specimen recovered there postdates 1492; one of the two must be the case, and they cannot both be. Settling that chronology, rather than any general preference for transmission or for invention, is what the Polynesian case now awaits.",
